1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,737
Two diagrams carry the same local episode label.

2
00:00:03,687 --> 00:00:08,237
Does that label alone tell us how many real subordinate units they command?

3
00:00:08,237 --> 00:00:12,725
These are teaching drawings, not authenticated official symbol meanings.

4
00:00:12,725 --> 00:00:17,200
The same local label can remain above two different EXAMPLE charts.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:24,462
It is an episode label only, not a verified FM military-symbol category or a direct inventory of real units.

6
00:00:24,462 --> 00:00:30,937
Three transparent film layers separate what the sign shows, what the legend claims, and what remains unknown.

7
00:00:30,937 --> 00:00:35,475
One-line test: symbol identity does not equal force strength.

8
00:00:35,475 --> 00:00:38,925
What extra evidence makes two maps comparable?

9
00:00:38,925 --> 00:00:42,350
Overlay one examines the symbol itself.

10
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:48,712
The Army's military-symbol publication describes conventions for representing units and their echelons.

11
00:00:48,712 --> 00:00:52,650
It is a visual language compressing information for a map.

12
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:57,200
Compression is useful, but it has specific and documented limits.

13
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:03,550
Start with an abstract corps emblem drawn as a source-traced symbol category, not a named active formation.

14
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:08,287
We omit all current deployments, real coordinates and tactical tasks.

15
00:01:08,287 --> 00:01:12,750
This page studies the information capacity of a published notation.

16
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:19,987
One mark can identify an echelon, while another annotation may communicate category under the chosen symbol convention.

17
00:01:19,987 --> 00:01:27,050
Neither silently supplies a personnel roster, vehicle inventory or permanent number of subordinate formations.

18
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:30,712
Those facts require documents of a different kind.

19
00:01:30,712 --> 00:01:35,262
Place the same abstract corps mark on two separate illustrative maps.

20
00:01:35,262 --> 00:01:38,487
The stamps are deliberately identical.

21
00:01:38,487 --> 00:01:42,625
We are not asserting that either map depicts a real organization.

22
00:01:42,625 --> 00:01:47,175
The illustration tests what a symbol alone could logically certify.

23
00:01:47,175 --> 00:01:53,612
First counterintuitive result: an unchanged icon need not imply an unchanged subordinate organization.

24
00:01:53,612 --> 00:02:02,425
An echelon mark can retain its classification meaning even if the actual set of subordinate or supporting formations differs under a documented arrangement.

25
00:02:02,425 --> 00:02:04,737
Open the boxed legend.

26
00:02:04,737 --> 00:02:09,050
Row one names the emblem as a classification, not a number.

27
00:02:09,050 --> 00:02:12,925
Row two records its reference document and convention.

28
00:02:12,925 --> 00:02:18,212
Row three identifies facts the image cannot reveal without a separate primary source.

29
00:02:18,212 --> 00:02:22,462
Military symbols also employ conventions for affiliation.

30
00:02:22,462 --> 00:02:30,025
We retain only the friendly affiliation colour supported by our symbol reference and avoid drawing adversary units as decoration.

31
00:02:30,025 --> 00:02:35,225
The focus is classification rather than battlefield planning or implied force strength.

32
00:02:35,225 --> 00:02:40,637
At the end of this first overlay, the label is only a label invented for this episode.

33
00:02:40,637 --> 00:02:47,175
Without the original symbol-table quotation, we do not give the marks a verified military echelon meaning.

34
00:02:47,175 --> 00:02:52,037
The second overlay compares two teaching diagrams with the same episode label.

35
00:02:52,037 --> 00:02:58,587
FM three dash zero describes corps and division headquarters functions in sections two eighty seven through eighty nine.

36
00:02:58,587 --> 00:03:02,400
It does not give a fixed personnel total for every unit.

37
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:10,425
FM three dash zero identifies a corps as a versatile echelon above brigade, able to operate at tactical and operational levels.

38
00:03:10,425 --> 00:03:14,375
Its headquarters may direct divisions and other formations.

39
00:03:14,375 --> 00:03:18,875
No exact real-world subordinate count follows from that description.

40
00:03:18,875 --> 00:03:22,950
These two overlays are EXAMPLES, not real organizations.

41
00:03:22,950 --> 00:03:27,112
One shows three illustrative boxes; the other shows five.

42
00:03:27,112 --> 00:03:36,650
FM three dash zero describes the division as a tactical formation that typically commands between two and five brigade combat teams, not an invariant personnel total.

43
00:03:36,650 --> 00:03:40,725
The same episode label sits above the two EXAMPLE charts.

44
00:03:40,725 --> 00:03:46,950
The examples illustrate that an unchanged teaching mark does not fix how many blank boxes appear beneath it.

45
00:03:46,950 --> 00:03:52,075
They are not official organization charts or reports of actual subordinate units.

46
00:03:52,075 --> 00:03:56,925
An organization chart needs a date and a sourced organizational context.

47
00:03:56,925 --> 00:04:04,025
FM three dash zero describes functions for corps and divisions, not the identities of units in our EXAMPLE diagrams.

48
00:04:04,025 --> 00:04:08,337
Neither illustration may be mistaken for current deployed strength.

49
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:14,212
More boxes make an EXAMPLE drawing visually larger but tell us nothing measured about personnel.

50
00:04:14,162 --> 00:04:20,300
FM three dash zero names several types of attached formations and changing division responsibilities.

51
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:24,737
A verified number of real soldiers would require separate evidence.

52
00:04:24,737 --> 00:04:30,637
Nothing here permits multiplying the number of corps signs by one division's estimated headcount.

53
00:04:30,637 --> 00:04:34,825
The earlier multiplication was a conditional teaching model.

54
00:04:34,825 --> 00:04:40,412
We cannot transform a categorical echelon mark into a table of fixed subordinate strengths.

55
00:04:40,412 --> 00:04:44,175
Overlay three examines headquarters relationships.

56
00:04:44,175 --> 00:04:49,112
Doctrine describes responsibilities and coordination among Army headquarters.

57
00:04:49,112 --> 00:04:56,037
Those organizational functions cannot be inferred solely by counting the echelon labels visible on our folded paper map.

58
00:04:56,037 --> 00:05:00,662
A line between two teaching labels is not an official command relationship.

59
00:05:00,662 --> 00:05:08,862
The marks and connectors shown here are labels for this episode only until their definitions can be verified against original military documents.

60
00:05:08,862 --> 00:05:14,350
Imagine a headquarters having additional supporting formations for a hypothetical task.

61
00:05:14,350 --> 00:05:19,262
A transparent layer can add blank units to demonstrate a changed depiction.

62
00:05:19,262 --> 00:05:25,562
We do not infer exact personnel totals or claim such a particular arrangement was operationally used.

63
00:05:25,562 --> 00:05:31,262
An EXAMPLE relationship line can move between boxes without identifying a real formation.

64
00:05:31,262 --> 00:05:40,912
FM three dash zero describes corps and division command functions, but it does not certify that our particular teaching connectors represent a real historical chain of command.

65
00:05:40,912 --> 00:05:45,587
The third comparison shows why quantity and echelon are different questions.

66
00:05:45,587 --> 00:05:52,737
FM three dash zero says divisions typically direct between two and five brigade combat teams, with other formations.

67
00:05:52,737 --> 00:05:57,425
The number of EXAMPLE boxes does not establish actual force strength.

68
00:05:57,425 --> 00:06:02,112
An accurate legend separates verified doctrine from teaching devices.

69
00:06:02,112 --> 00:06:09,600
Here FM three dash zero supports general corps and division functions, and the extra connecting lines remain labelled EXAMPLE.

70
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:13,812
They are not source-traced operational orders or real unit strengths.

71
00:06:13,812 --> 00:06:17,375
Read the source legend before interpreting the arrow.

72
00:06:17,375 --> 00:06:22,512
A publication defines a mark; an accompanying text explains how it is employed.

73
00:06:22,512 --> 00:06:28,800
Source labels appear next to their respective claims, not in unconnected decorative corners of the paper.

74
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:33,587
The labels on this map do not prove a real unit's echelon or total strength.

75
00:06:33,587 --> 00:06:36,825
They are teaching marks made for this episode.

76
00:06:36,825 --> 00:06:43,050
Never read them as authoritative military symbols without checking their original published definitions.

77
00:06:43,050 --> 00:06:51,162
The final overlay removes all hypothetical subordinate units and leaves a single original echelon marker beside a checklist of unknown fields.

78
00:06:51,162 --> 00:06:57,625
That removal prevents anyone from mistaking our three-versus-five illustration for a measured Army organization.

79
00:06:57,625 --> 00:07:01,200
Legend item one is the symbol classification.

80
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:05,200
Its source is the original military-symbol publication.

81
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:07,387
Type: definition.

82
00:07:07,387 --> 00:07:14,012
Its value is not a troop count, and the field for personnel remains empty instead of receiving an attractive number.

83
00:07:14,012 --> 00:07:21,162
Item two describes the functional role of a headquarters and the kinds of formations identified in the relevant Army manual.

84
00:07:21,162 --> 00:07:24,100
Type: organizational context.

85
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:29,375
It does not claim a universal branching factor or a single fixed organization.

86
00:07:29,375 --> 00:07:34,275
Item three records the teaching diagrams: three boxes and five boxes.

87
00:07:34,275 --> 00:07:39,000
Those values are assumed illustration counts, not observed force strengths.

88
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,287
Every such row is marked EXAMPLE; neither count is attributed to an actual corps.

89
00:07:44,287 --> 00:07:51,287
Item four is a list of unknowns: subordinate composition, applicable date, real identity and total personnel.

90
00:07:51,287 --> 00:07:54,562
Without published evidence, each stays blank.

91
00:07:54,562 --> 00:07:59,337
An empty evidence field is not zero and must not be filled by intuition.

92
00:07:59,337 --> 00:08:09,450
Three changes could alter a detailed command diagram without altering its echelon symbol: the represented date, the set of formations considered, and the documented relationships.

93
00:08:09,450 --> 00:08:14,350
This is an interpretation rule, not a prediction about a current formation.

94
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:18,225
Return to the two identical stamps from the opening.

95
00:08:18,175 --> 00:08:22,912
Both can be correctly classified yet insufficient for direct comparison.

96
00:08:22,912 --> 00:08:29,125
Sources establish what the icon means, while separate records establish the structure it cannot show.

97
00:08:29,125 --> 00:08:33,800
No real positions, tactical orders or live unit lists are shown.

98
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:37,612
The map teaches the limits of institutional notation.

99
00:08:37,612 --> 00:08:45,712
Keeping source-defined, hypothetical and unknown information separate is the only way to prevent polished imagery from producing false certainty.

100
00:08:45,712 --> 00:08:50,862
Next in Grid Square Brief: why command relationship lines need their own sources.

